    re_h: 'The recipes and what they give up',
    re_lede: 'The study’s named recipes at the study’s conventions (maintenance 1%, taker 0.05%), then the realistic case: venue maintenance and observed funding together.',
    th_recipe: 'Recipe', th_be_grp: 'Break-even funding', th_be: '2010–26', th_last3: 'Latest 3-year window',
    rc_recipe_a: 'A (0.20, 2x, monthly) · maint. 1% · +10%', rc_recipe_b: 'B (0.25, 2x, monthly) · maint. 1% · +10%', rc_recipe_c: 'C (1/3, 3x, monthly) · maint. 1% · +10%',
    rc_recipe_base: 'Base (1/3, 5x, monthly) · maint. 1% · +10%', rc_recipe_qqq: 'QQQ',
    re_b1_html: '<strong>Each +1% of funding adds about +0.19 points to A and +0.23 to B</strong>; against the same-volatility mix: at zero funding A −0.5, B −0.7; at +5% both +0.4; at +14% +2.2 / +2.6. At 2x TQQQ must rise 48.5% within a month (1% maintenance; about 46% at 2.5%) to wipe the margin, which it did in only two months in 16.7 years (September 2010 and April 2026, the latter the largest in the sample at +53%); at 5x the line is 18.8%.',
    re_b2_html: '<strong>A’s edge holds from any start date</strong> (+1.4 / +1.4 / +1.3 from 2010 / 2015 / 2020) and in 100% of the 55 rolling 3-year windows at +10% (worst +0.7), 94.5% at +5%, 21.8% at 0. But the 55 windows hold about five independent periods, the recipe was chosen on the same sample, and the mix’s QQQ share is matched with hindsight. The zero-funding edge falls as T-bill rates rise (correlation −0.78 with the window’s mean rate): about 0 for windows ending 2014, −1.15 points for 2026. A’s break-even across the 55 windows: minimum 0, median 1.6%, maximum 6.5% (window ending 2026-05).',
    re_b3_html: '<strong>What they give up</strong>: $1 in February 2010 became $4.72 (A) or $3.46 (B) against $18.89 for QQQ, 9.5–11.5 points of return a year, mostly in rallies (2023: QQQ +55%, A +21%, B +15%; in the 15 up years A trailed QQQ by 14.3 points on average, in the 2 down years it led by 13.9).',
    re_b4_html: '<strong>Against other ways to be smoother</strong> (A at +10%): same-volatility less spot (37% QQQ) +1.4; same worst fall (31.5% QQQ) +2.5, but both matches rest on the 2022 episode (in 2010–2019 the same-drawdown edge is only +1.7); the 200-day-line switch +2.1 to +2.2 (driven by the rule’s 2010–2019 whipsaws; from 2020 −0.1, and −2.1 at zero funding); QQQ+IEF +1.2 (from 2020 +4.9, in 2010–2019 −1.5). Over 2010–2026 no QQQ+IEF mix had a worst fall shallower than about −20.6% and no QQQ+TLT mix shallower than −34.5%, but that is the 2020–2023 bond bear market; in 2010–2019 QQQ+IEF mixes reached −5.3% and beat A by 1.4 points at the same drawdown. 60/40 QQQ/IEF (12.7% / 12.1% / −28.2%) is matched in volatility by a hedge ratio of only 0.12 (13.0% / 12.1% / −19.9%; 12.4% at +5%), a book with net beta about 0.57, whose edge is inside the funding uncertainty.',
    re_b5_html: '<strong>Nasdaq-100-like spot only.</strong> XLK (beta 1.04, correlation 0.97): A +0.7. SPY (0.77, 0.93): the base hedge −0.4% a year with a −26.1% worst fall, 7.4 below the mix; A −1.3 (−0.7 after scaling the hedge by beta to 0.153); no SPY variant beats the mix at funding of 10% or less (28 of 384 do at +15% to +20%, by at most +0.9). BTC (correlation 0.23): the one-third hedge cuts volatility only from 65.5% to 56.9% and the worst fall from −83% to −79%, 13.7 below the mix. Single stocks NVDA / MSFT / AVGO / TSLA: below the same-volatility stock/T-bill mix at every setting, by 1.6 to 18.5 points. An index futures hedge would not fix this either: it removes only 18–42% of a single stock’s daily volatility (§08).',
    re_b6_html: '<strong>Modelling conventions</strong>: crediting funding to the isolated margin (which lifts the liquidation line) rather than to cash favours the 5x base by 0.31 points; closing and reopening the whole short at each monthly reset rather than trading the change costs it 0.35 (2x 0.32, hedge 0.2 0.23); reopening at the open on gap-through liquidations +0.12 (10x +1.09). All four together: +0.14 at 5x, +0.34 at 2x, +1.37 at 10x, +0.08 for hedge 0.2; they “cancel” at 5x only. Trading only the change would lower recipe A’s break-even by about 1 point.',
    rc_h: 'The realistic case: venue maintenance and observed funding together',
    rc_intro: 'Each specialist varied one assumption at a time; here both are set at once with the study’s simulator (monthly reset, taker 0.06%, funding on calendar days). Each cell: return a year / vs the same-volatility mix / liquidations a year / worst fall.',
    ctl_rc_funding: 'Funding a year',
    rc_win_long: '2010-02 to 2026-10 (T-bills {tb} a year)', rc_win_short: '2023-10 to 2026-10 (T-bills {tb} a year)',
    th_maint: 'Maintenance {m}',
    rc_row_ref: '(reference) 1/3, 5x',
    rc_cell: '{cagr} / {vs} / {liq} / {mdd}',
    more_numbers: 'the numbers',
    rc_p2_lead: 'At +10% funding the edges are larger over 2010–2026 but not over the last three years; the cost of idle margin is already counted.',
    rc_p2: 'At funding +10% and 2.5% maintenance the edges over 2010–2026 are +0.8 / +0.4 (1/3 at 2x / 3x) and +1.3 / +1.1 (0.2 at 2x / 3x), but only −0.7 / −1.5 and +0.5 / +0.1 over the last three years. The cost of idle margin is already inside these figures (margin earns nothing): hedge 1/3 at 2x posts 13.6% of NAV, 0.2% a year at 2010–2026 rates and 0.6% at today’s 4.3%; if the 5% cash reserve must also sit on the exchange in USDT, add about 0.2%. Measured against Nasdaq-100 futures instead (§08), each of these “vs mix” figures is about 0.17–0.26 points lower (same daily beta, futures carry 0.25%; 0.07–0.10 at a carry of 0.10%): recipe A at +5% leads futures by about 0.2 points (same daily beta) over 2010–2026 and trails by about 0.6–0.7 over the last three years, so its “wash” is slightly negative today.',
    rc_p3_html: '<strong>Read together:</strong> at the maintenance and funding the venues actually show, the one-third hedge earns about T-bills and trails holding less QQQ by 0.6–3.3 points (2–5 over the last three years); the 0.2 hedge at 2x is a wash (within ±0.5 of the mix) and beats it only if funding stays at the October–November run rate (10% or more). Order of the mechanical corrections: maintenance tier (5x: −2.7 points), funding level (0.2–0.3 points per point), 24/7 trading (−0.25), fees (−0.06 at the documented rate). Measured against Nasdaq-100 futures (§08) instead of the mix, each of these “vs mix” gaps (not the corrections) is about 0.17–0.26 points lower (same daily beta, carry 0.25%; 0.07–0.10 at 0.10%), so recipe A’s wash is slightly negative today.',

